We are now performing the FINAL RELEASE READINESS / QA REVIEW for the Subscription Billing & Usage-Metering System.

This is an interview take-home assignment submission.

IMPORTANT:
- Do NOT add new business features.
- Do NOT redesign the architecture.
- Do NOT create new modules.
- Do NOT change working behavior unnecessarily.
- Do NOT commit or push Git changes.
- Treat the current implementation as feature complete.
- Only fix genuine correctness, test, configuration, documentation, or submission-readiness issues.

==================================================
1. PROJECT HEALTH CHECK
==================================================

Inspect the complete repository.

Verify:

- Laravel application boots correctly.
- Composer dependencies are valid.
- Environment configuration is documented.
- Database configuration is valid.
- Cache configuration is valid.
- Queue configuration is valid.
- Application routes load successfully.
- No fatal configuration issues exist.

Run appropriate commands such as:

php artisan about
php artisan route:list
php artisan migrate:status
php artisan test

Only use commands supported by the current project.

==================================================
2. CLEAN DATABASE VALIDATION
==================================================

Verify that the application can build its database from a clean state.

Use the project's normal migration workflow.

If safe for the development/test environment, validate:

php artisan migrate:fresh

Then run the complete test suite.

Do not execute destructive commands against any production database.

Verify:

- all migrations execute in correct order
- foreign keys work
- unique constraints work
- indexes exist
- no migration depends on manual database changes.

==================================================
3. TEST SUITE
==================================================

Run the complete test suite.

Verify all tests pass.

Review test output for:

- failures
- warnings
- skipped tests
- risky tests
- unexpected deprecations.

Do not hide failures.

If a failure is caused by a genuine application bug, fix it.

If a test is incorrectly asserting implementation details, improve the test while preserving business coverage.

Do not weaken assertions simply to achieve green tests.

==================================================
4. ROUTE REVIEW
==================================================

Run:

php artisan route:list

Review all application routes.

Verify:

- no accidental debug/test routes
- no duplicate routes
- correct HTTP methods
- correct API prefixes
- expected controller/action mapping
- dashboard route exists
- usage endpoint exists.

Do not add undocumented routes.

==================================================
5. API CONTRACT REVIEW
==================================================

Review the important endpoints against their implementation.

Verify request validation and response structure.

Important areas:

POST /api/v1/usage

GET /api/v1/merchants/{id}/dashboard

And any other implemented endpoints required by the assignment.

Ensure README examples match actual request/response structures.

Do not invent API responses in documentation.

==================================================
6. MULTI-TENANCY FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Perform a final audit of tenant boundaries.

Review all queries touching:

- customers
- plans
- subscriptions
- subscription_segments
- usage_events
- daily_usage
- invoices
- dashboard.

Verify merchant_id/tenant ownership is correctly enforced wherever required.

Pay special attention to:

- route parameters
- service methods
- queued jobs
- aggregation
- billing
- dashboard.

Do not introduce a new tenancy abstraction at this stage.

==================================================
7. BILLING FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Review representative billing scenarios.

Verify:

1. Normal subscription.
2. Usage below allowance.
3. Usage exactly equal to allowance.
4. Usage above allowance.
5. Mid-cycle subscription start.
6. Mid-cycle upgrade.
7. Mid-cycle downgrade.
8. Multiple subscription segments.
9. Zero usage.
10. Duplicate billing execution.

Verify invoice totals manually against the documented formulas.

Do not change the formula unless the existing implementation is genuinely incorrect.

==================================================
8. USAGE INGESTION FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Verify:

- validation
- tenant ownership
- idempotency
- duplicate retry handling
- conflicting idempotency key handling
- database uniqueness
- rate limiting
- lightweight synchronous processing.

Ensure the usage API does NOT synchronously perform expensive billing/aggregation work.

==================================================
9. AGGREGATION FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Verify:

usage_events
        ↓
chunked processing
        ↓
database aggregation
        ↓
daily_usage

Ensure:

- repeated aggregation is safe
- late events can be handled
- duplicate usage is not double counted
- large datasets are not loaded entirely into memory.

==================================================
10. CACHE FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Verify:

- pricing cache key
- TTL
- cache lookup
- DB fallback
- invalidation after pricing update
- historical pricing independence.

Ensure billing does not accidentally depend on the current cached plan price for historical segments.

==================================================
11. DASHBOARD FINAL CHECK
==================================================

Verify:

- top 5 customers by current-month usage
- projected overage revenue
- >50% month-over-month drop
- zero previous-month usage handling
- tenant isolation
- no obvious N+1 queries.

Do not optimize prematurely.

==================================================
12. PERFORMANCE / SCALE REVIEW
==================================================

Review the implementation from the perspective of:

50,00,000+ usage_event rows.

Verify:

- appropriate indexes exist
- ingestion avoids unnecessary joins
- aggregation is chunked
- aggregation uses DB-side SUM/GROUP BY
- daily_usage reduces repeated raw-event scanning
- billing works from aggregated data
- dashboard uses aggregated data where appropriate.

Ensure README explains future:

- partitioning
- archival
- read replicas
- horizontal queue workers

as future scaling options rather than falsely claiming they are implemented.

==================================================
13. SECURITY REVIEW
==================================================

Perform final practical security checks.

Look for:

- hardcoded credentials
- committed .env
- API keys
- passwords
- debug dumps
- dd()
- dump()
- temporary test endpoints
- unsafe raw SQL
- mass assignment issues
- unvalidated input
- cross-tenant access.

Remove only genuine accidental/debug artifacts.

Do not introduce unrelated security infrastructure.

==================================================
14. GIT / REPOSITORY CLEANLINESS
==================================================

Inspect:

git status
git diff
git ls-files

Verify repository does NOT contain:

- .env
- credentials
- local database files
- IDE-specific temporary files
- logs
- cache files
- node_modules if not required
- vendor if the project's normal Git strategy excludes it.

Verify .gitignore is appropriate.

Do not remove required project files.

==================================================
15. PROMPTS DIRECTORY
==================================================

Verify:

/prompts

contains the actual AI prompt screenshots used during development.

Expected logical sequence:

01-project-foundation
02-database-schema
03-usage-api
04-usage-aggregation
05-billing-engine
06-cache-dashboard
07-integration-tests
08-final-hardening
09-final-qa

If the current prompt screenshot is not yet saved, do NOT fabricate it.

Only report missing screenshots.

==================================================
16. README FINAL ACCURACY CHECK
==================================================

Read the complete README and verify every statement against the actual code.

Especially verify:

- endpoint names
- commands
- class names
- database tables
- cache behavior
- queue behavior
- billing formula
- proration formula
- assumptions
- test commands.

Remove claims about functionality that is not implemented.

The README must be honest.

==================================================
17. ADD A FINAL "SUBMISSION CHECKLIST"
==================================================

If not already present, add a concise checklist:

- [ ] Database migrations verified
- [ ] Tests passing
- [ ] API endpoints verified
- [ ] Idempotency implemented
- [ ] Queue aggregation implemented
- [ ] Billing/proration tested
- [ ] Mid-cycle plan changes tested
- [ ] Cache/invalidation implemented
- [ ] Dashboard implemented
- [ ] Rate limiting implemented
- [ ] Multi-tenant isolation tested
- [ ] README completed
- [ ] Prompt screenshots included
- [ ] No secrets committed.

Use the actual project state.

==================================================
18. FINAL TEST COMMANDS
==================================================

Run the project's appropriate final validation commands.

At minimum:

php artisan test

Also run relevant project formatter/static analysis commands if configured.

Run:

php artisan route:list

and any safe migration validation required by the project.

Do not run destructive production commands.

==================================================
19. FINAL REPORT
==================================================

At the end, provide a concise release-readiness report:

A. Application status
B. Test result
C. Migration result
D. Route result
E. Security result
F. Multi-tenancy result
G. Billing result
H. Scalability review result
I. README result
J. Prompt-log result
K. Remaining issues, if any

Classify remaining issues as:

- BLOCKER
- IMPORTANT
- OPTIONAL

The repository should only be considered submission-ready if there are no BLOCKER issues.

Do not commit or push changes.